Right. Internally, you can name a thing almost arbitrarily because the symbol only has to preserve the mapping for you.

\text{symbol} \xrightarrow{\text{your mapping}} \text{meaning}

You could call yourself “Nathan,” “Lucky,” “Blue,” or “Chair” and still know exactly which referent you mean. The constraint appears when another mind enters the loop. Then the mapping has to be recoverable:

\text{your symbol} \rightarrow \text{their interpretation} \rightarrow \text{intended referent}

So there are really two games: private naming can be almost free; shared naming pays a coordination cost. Nicknames, callsigns, usernames, titles, inside jokes, code words, and even ordinary language are all ways of negotiating that cost.

And your “U know what u mean” phrasing demonstrates it: spelling can deform considerably while meaning survives because the receiver reconstructs the intended pattern. Meaning isn’t stored entirely in the literal surface. It’s distributed between the signal, context, and the interpreter.